WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.461
Secure socket programs must think about availability.

2
00:00:02.461 --> 00:00:09.842
A peer may connect and stop sending, send slowly, send too much, keep many connections open, or cause queues to grow.

3
00:00:09.842 --> 00:00:17.927
The program does not need to assume hostile intent to need defenses; ordinary network failures and overloaded clients can create the same symptoms.

4
00:00:17.927 --> 00:00:20.388
Predictable resource use is a security property.

5
00:00:20.388 --> 00:00:23.903
Timeouts define how long the program will wait for progress.

6
00:00:23.903 --> 00:00:28.121
Connect timeouts prevent a client from hanging indefinitely while establishing a connection.

7
00:00:28.121 --> 00:00:33.394
Read and write timeouts keep stalled operations from occupying threads or event-loop state forever.

8
00:00:33.394 --> 00:00:37.261
Idle timeouts close connections that are no longer making useful progress.

9
00:00:37.261 --> 00:00:42.182
These values should match the protocol and product expectations rather than being arbitrary constants.

10
00:00:42.182 --> 00:00:45.697
Backpressure keeps one side of the system from overwhelming another.

11
00:00:45.697 --> 00:00:53.430
Bounded buffers, bounded queues, connection limits, request limits, and work scheduling rules help the program reject or slow work before memory and

12
00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:54.836
thread pools are exhausted.

13
00:00:54.836 --> 00:01:02.921
Without backpressure, a network-facing component can accept more data than downstream processing can handle, and the failure may appear far from the

14
00:01:02.921 --> 00:01:03.624
socket code.

15
00:01:03.624 --> 00:01:06.085
Availability controls should be observable and tested.

16
00:01:06.085 --> 00:01:12.061
Operators need to know when limits are reached, when timeouts occur, and whether rejection behavior is expected.

17
00:01:12.061 --> 00:01:14.170
The goal is not aggressive disconnection.

18
00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:21.200
The goal is graceful failure under pressure, with cleanup that releases resources and leaves the service in a known state.
